{
 "id": "l25",
 "label": "25",
 "title": "Saán and násaán: where",
 "summary": "Saán for where an action happens, násaán for where a person or thing is, and how to answer each",
 "minutes": 30,
 "goals": [
  "Choose between saán and násaán",
  "Answer a saán question with sa + place, or díto, díyan, doón",
  "Answer a násaán question with nása + place, or nárito, náriyan, nároón",
  "Place a pronoun between saán and the verb"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Two words for where",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Lesson 9 listed **saán** and **násaán** among the 14 question words. Both mean *where*, but each one needs its own kind of answer."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Question word",
      "Asks for…",
      "Answer with"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[saán|other]]",
       "the location of an **action**",
       "**sa** + name of place, or díto / díyan / doón"
      ],
      [
       "[[násaán|other]]",
       "the location of a **person or thing**",
       "**nása** + name of place, or nárito / náriyan / nároón"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Saán or násaán?"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "english",
     "title": "A quick test",
     "t": "Is there an action (went, eat, read, speak)? Use **saán**. Are you only asking where someone or something *is*? Use **násaán**."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Spelling",
     "t": "Lesson 9 and this lesson's vocabulary list write **nasaán**. The rest of this lesson (title, explanations and sentences) writes **násaán**. Both are the same word."
    }
   ]
  },
